\begin{quote}
The algebraic properties of the ring \emph{Rq} =
\emph{Zq}{[}x{]}\emph{/}(x\emph{n}+ 1), where \emph{n} is a power of 2,
depend on the prime \emph{q}. For efficiency, one often takes \emph{q} =
1 mod (2\emph{n}), which results in the polynomial x\emph{n}+ 1
splitting into \emph{n} linear factors modulo \emph{q}. Operations
within the ring can then be performed extremely efficiently using the
number theory transform. On the other hand, one sometimes wants the ring
to be `\,'almost a field''. In particular, it is sometimes desirable for
the ring to have many invertible elements. While there do not exist
\emph{q} that will make \emph{Rq} a field, using \emph{q} = 3 mod 8 (as
first suggested in {[}SSTX09{]}) has the effect that x\emph{n}+ 1
factors into two irreducible polynomials of degree \emph{n/}2 and so the
ring \emph{Rq} contains \emph{qn−}2\emph{qn/}2+ 1 invertible elements.
By the Chinese Remainder theorem, it is also easy to see that all
elements of degree less than \emph{n/}2 are invertible.

We have not seen this used in previous works, but it turns out that
setting \emph{q} = 5 mod 8 may be even more convenient. Modulo such a
\emph{q}, the polynomial x\emph{n}+ 1 also factors into two irreducible

2 We point out that since Z\emph{q} is a finite group, these do not
