polynomials of degree \emph{n/}2. And in addition to all elements of
degree less than \emph{n/}2 being invertible, one can also show that all
elements (of degree up to \emph{n}) with small coefficients are
invertible as well. We present the proof of this statement in Lemma 2.2.
To the best of our knowledge, this lemma was first proven in an
unpublished manuscript of Lyubashevsky and Xagawa.

\textbf{Lemma 2.1 ({[}LN86{]}, special case of Theorem 3.35, page 88).}
\emph{Let q} = 5(mod 8) \emph{be prime and r be an integer such that
r}2= \emph{−}1(mod \emph{q})\emph{. Then for all positive integers κ,
the polynomials} x2\emph{κ−r and} x2\emph{κ}+ \emph{r are irreducible
over} Z\emph{q}{[}x{]}\emph{. And in particular, the complete
factorization into irreducibles over} Z\emph{q}{[}x{]} \emph{of the
polynomial} x2\emph{κ}+1+ 1 \emph{is} x2\emph{κ}+1+ 1 =
(x2\emph{κ−r})(x2\emph{κ}+ \emph{r}) mod \emph{q.} \textbf{Lemma 2.2.}
\emph{Let Rq} = Z\emph{q}{[}x{]}\emph{/}(\emph{xn}+ 1) \emph{where n
\textgreater{}} 1 \emph{is a power of} 2 \emph{and q is a prime
congruent to} 5(mod 8)\emph{. This ring has exactly}
2\emph{qn/}2\emph{−}1 \emph{elements without an inverse. Moreover, every
non-zero polynomial} a \emph{in Rq with ∥}a\emph{∥∞\textless{}}
\emph{q/}2 \emph{has an inverse.}

\emph{Proof.} In all that follows, the reduction modulo \emph{q} will be
implicit. By Lemma 2.1, x\emph{n}+ 1 =
(x\emph{n/}2\emph{−r})(x\emph{n/}2+\emph{r}) where \emph{r}2= \emph{−}1
and x\emph{n/}2\emph{±r} are irreducible. Any element a \emph{∈Rq} can
be written as a = a0 + x\emph{n/}2a1\emph{,} where a0\emph{,} a1 are
polynomials in Z{[}x{]} of degree less than \emph{n/}2. Then the Chinese
remainder decomposition of a is

\emph{CRT}(a) = (a mod (x\emph{n/}2\emph{−r})\emph{,} a mod
(x\emph{n/}2+ \emph{r})) = (a0 + \emph{r}a1\emph{,} a0
\emph{−r}a1)\emph{.}

If a is not invertible, it means that either a0 + \emph{r}a1 = 0 or a0
\emph{−r}a1 = 0. If a1 = 0, then a0 = 0 and a is the zero polynomial. If
a1 \emph{̸}= 0, then some coefficient of a0, say \emph{α}0, must be
equal to \emph{±rα}1, where \emph{α}1 is a non-zero coefficient of a1.
Therefore we have \emph{α}2\emph{α}2 0+ \emph{α}2 1= 0. But since we
assumed that \emph{\textbar α}0\emph{\textbar,
\textbar α}1\emph{\textbar{} \textless{}} \emph{q/}2, this is not
possible, and thus proves 0= (\emph{±rα}1)2 = \emph{−α}2 1. In other
words,

the second part of the lemma by contradiction. The first part of the
lemma follows from the fact that CRT is a bijection and all the elements
without an inverse must be 0 modulo at least one of x\emph{n/}2\emph{±
r}. \emph{⊔}

\textbf{2.3 Lattices and the Discrete Gaussian Distribution}

A full-rank integer lattice \emph{Λ} of dimension \emph{n} is an
additive subgroup of Z\emph{n}that is generated by some basis \textbf{B}
= {[}\textbf{b}1 \emph{\textbar{} . . . \textbar{}}
