\begin{quote}
The idea in {[}Lyu09,Lyu12{]} was to construct a \emph{Σ}-protocol with
the main twist being that the prover does not always output the result.
In particular, the protocols use rejection sampling to tailor the
distribution so that it does not depend on the secret \textbf{s}. This
rejection sampling can be done by making the resulting distribution
uniform in a box (as in {[}Lyu09{]}), or the more efficient approach in
{[}Lyu12{]} of making it a discrete Gaussian. The interactive protocol
is then converted to a non-interactive one in the random-oracle model
{[}BR93{]} using the Fiat-Shamir technique {[}FS86{]}. This combined
technique is sometimes referred to as ``Fiat-Shamir with Aborts''. While
the focus of the earlier works was on signature schemes
{[}Lyu09,Lyu12{]}, here we formalize the approach as a NIZK proof system
for ``relaxed'' relations, i.e., where extraction yields a witness from
a different language than is used to produce the proof.

\emph{Relaxed NIZK proof systems.} We adapt the definition of
non-interactive zero-knowledge (NIZK) proof systems in the random-oracle
model of Faust et al. {[}FKMV12{]} to a relaxed setting, where the
extracted witness could come from a different language. A \emph{relaxed
NIZK proof system} for languages \emph{L,}¯\emph{L} is a couple of
algorithms (PH\emph{,} VH) where the prover algorithm PH, on input a
language member \emph{x} and witness \emph{w} such that (\emph{x, w})
\emph{∈RL}, returns a proof \emph{π}, and where the verification
algorithm VH, on input \emph{x} and \emph{π}, outputs 0 or 1 indicating
that the proof is invalid or valid, respectively. Let S be \$\\
a NIZK simulator modeled as a stateful algorithm that operates in two
modes: (\emph{h, st′})\emph{←}S(1\emph{, st, q}) returns the simulated
output \emph{h}1 to a random-oracle query H(\emph{q}) together with an
updated state \$\\
\emph{st′}, while (\emph{π, st′})\emph{←}S(2\emph{, st, x}) returns a
simulated proof for \emph{x}. The correctness and zero-knowledge
properties below are identitical to those of standard (i.e.,
non-relaxed) NIZK proof systems. The simulation soundness property has
been relaxed to allow \emph{x∗}to be in the extended language ¯instead
of \emph{L}.

\textbf{Correctness.} Correctness requires that VH(\emph{x,} PH(\emph{x,
w})) = 1 with probability one for all (\emph{x, w}) \emph{∈} \emph{RL}.

