Section 2.6 describes a relaxed NIZK proof system for the languages
\emph{L} and ¯\emph{L} of the verifiable encryption scheme in Section
3.1. Faust et al. {[}FKMV12{]} showed that for the Fiat-Shamir
transfor-mation of a \emph{Σ}-protocol, the unbounded non-interactive
zero-knowledge property follows from the honest-verifier zero-knowledge
(HVZK) property of the underlying \emph{Σ}-protocol, which we proved in
Section 2.6. Faust et al. also showed that the unbounded simulation
soundness property holds for NIZK proof systems if the underlying
\emph{Σ}-protocol is HVZK and has quasi-unique responses. An easy
adaptation of their proof shows that it also holds for relaxed NIZK
proof systems. We showed in Section 2.6 that the underlying
\emph{Σ}-protocol indeed has quasi-unique responses, so the relaxed NIZK
proof system is unbounded simulation-sound.

A variation of the signing protocol from {[}Lyu12{]} is given in
Algorithm 1. It was shown in that work that the output \textbf{z} is
distributed according to \emph{DRk,}0\emph{,σ}. In particular, the
rejection sampling stem on line 4 has the effect that the distribution
of \textbf{z} is independent of the secret vector \textbf{s} (and the
challenge c). This algorithm is therefore honest-verifier zero knowledge
since a simulator can \$ \$\\
simply output \textbf{z}\emph{←DRk,}0\emph{,σ, c}\\
\emph{←C} and program c = H(\textbf{A}\emph{,} \textbf{t}\emph{,}
\textbf{Az} \emph{−}\textbf{t}c mod \textbf{q}).3We also make the
observation that one does not need to use the same modulus \emph{q} for
every row of the relation in (8). One can instead use a different
modulus for each row, and we represent this in the protocol by a vector
\textbf{q} -- having different moduli is crucial to the application in
this paper.

We also need simulation soundness {[}Sah99{]}, meaning that an adversary
cannot create proofs of incorrect statements, even after seeing
simulated proofs of incorrect statements. Faust et al. {[}FKMV12{]}
showed that Fiat-Shamir proofs are simulation-sound if the underlying
three-move protocol is honest-verifier zero-knowledge and has
``quasi-unique responses'', meaning that an adversary can-not create two
accepting transcripts that are different only in the response value. For
our proof system, this translates into finding \textbf{z} \emph{̸}=
\textbf{z}\emph{′}such that \textbf{Az} = \textbf{Az}\emph{′}mod
\emph{q} be hard. Finding such \textbf{z}\emph{,}
\textbf{z}\emph{′}would imply that \textbf{A}(\textbf{z}
\emph{−}\textbf{z}\emph{′}) = 0 mod \emph{q} where \textbf{A} is the
matrix in Equation 7. Thus, there is either a non-zero tuple (y1\emph{,}
y2) \emph{∈Rq} with \emph{l∞}norm less than 12\emph{σ} such that ay1 +
