The second issue is how to find a valid c. In particular, if we would
like the proof of knowledge to be ``one-shot'', then the challenge space
should be exponentially large, and so it is impractical to simply try
all the possible c (of which there are actually even more than in the
challenge space). We show in Lemma 3.2, however, that the decryptor can
try random c (there is some relation between\emph{π} and which ¯c should
be tried), and then the \emph{expected} number of tries is essentially
the number of random oracle queries that the prover makes when
constructing \emph{π}, where the probability is taken over the
randomness of the random oracle (modeled as a random function) and the
coins of the decryptor. Algorithm 5 is the decryption algorithm that
guesses a random c\emph{′}from \emph{C}, constructs \textbf{v}\\
c = c\emph{−}c\emph{′}, where c is part of the proof \emph{π}, and then
checks whether c \textbf{w} mod \emph{q} is a valid ciphertext

(actually \emph{k} valid ciphertexts because the plaintext \textbf{m} is
encrypted as \emph{k} independent plaintexts). If it is, then it
decrypts it, and otherwise it guesses a new random c\emph{′}.

