\emph{Completeness.} Completeness follows from the completeness of the
proof system of Section 2.6.

\emph{Simulatability.} The simulator Sim creates a Ring-LWE encryption
{[} \textbf{v} ; \textbf{w} {]} of \textbf{m} = 1 using the scheme of
Section 2.5 and runs the zero-knowledge simulator for the proof system
of Section 2.6 to create a valid-looking proof (c\emph{,} \textbf{z})
for (\textbf{B}\emph{,} \textbf{u}). The indistinguishability from the
real proof follows from the IND-CPA security of Ring-LWE encryption and
the zero-knowledge property of the proof system.

\textbf{3.5 Decryption Running Time}

Even though the running time of the decryption algorithm is unbounded in
principle, we show that its \emph{expected} running time is proportional
to the number of times that the adversary queries the random oracle.
More precisely, we show that if an adversary uses \emph{q}H
random-oracle queries to construct a ciphertext, then the probability
that the decryption algorithm requires more than \emph{α·q}H iterations
is less than 1\emph{/α}.

We prove the above information-theoretic statement for any adversary
\emph{A}, and we can therefore limit the analysis to deterministic
adversaries, since the coins that maximize the adversary's success can
always be hardwired into its code.

