\begin{quote}
\textbf{Time}
\end{quote}

If one would like to put a limit on how much computational time
decryption takes in the worst case,

then the idea is to reduce the size of the challenge space and repeat
the proof-of-knowledge protocol

19

\begin{quote}
from Algorithm 3 in parallel \emph{α} times using the standard approach.
This protocol is described in Algorithm 6. The verification procedure
simply checks if all the \emph{α} copies are verified correctly.

One can show that with probability approximately 1
\emph{−\textbar C\textbar−α}, there will be at least one c for
\textbf{v}\\
which c is a valid ciphertext satisfying Lemma 3.1, and so one simply
needs to find it (and \textbf{w}

\textbf{v}\\
check its validity) analogously to the way c was found in Algorithm 5.
The main difference \textbf{w}\\
is that the challenge space is no longer exponentially large, and so one
can search through all the c = c(\emph{i})\emph{−}c\emph{′}in time
\emph{α · \textbar C\textbar{}}. This procedure is described in
Algorithm 8. If one wants to also maintain the relationship between the
number of random oracle queries of the prover to the number of
decryption tries, one could ``dovetail'' between Algorithm 8 which
systematically goes through all c\emph{′∈C} with one that randomly
guesses them at random.

The trade-off between having the decryption running time be
upper-bounded by \emph{α · \textbar C\textbar{}} is that the proof of
knowledge is now approximately \emph{α} times larger.

