\begin{quote}
Many applications require a verifiable ciphertext to hide the encrypted
witness, even when the adversary has access to decryptions of other
ciphertexts. As a natural analog of indistinguishability under
chosen-ciphertext attack (IND-CCA) for standard public-key encryption
schemes, we define chosen-ciphertext simulatability and describe a
construction that satisfies it.

Our construction essentially follows the Naor-Yung paradigm {[}NY90{]}
where the sender encrypts the message twice under different public keys
and adds a non-interactive zero-knowledge (NIZK) proof that both
ciphertexts encrypt the same message. Naor and Yung only proved their
appro-ach secure under non-adaptive chosen-ciphertext (CCA1), but Sahai
{[}Sah99{]} later showed that if the NIZK proof is simulation-sound,
then the resulting encryption scheme is secure against adap-tive
chosen-ciphertext (CCA2) attacks. Faust et al. {[}FKMV12{]} showed that
Fiat-Shamir proofs
\end{quote}

21

are simulation-sound in the random-oracle model if the underlying proof
system has quasi-unique

responses.

\begin{quote}
Furthermore, because the verifiable encryption scheme for a CPA-secure
encryption scheme
\end{quote}

already includes a NIZK, this conversion from CPA to CCA2 security is
not too expensive, increasing

the size of the proof and ciphertext by factors less than 2 (see (22)).

\emph{Chosen-ciphertext simulatability.} We say that a relaxed
verifiable encryption scheme (Kg\emph{,} Enc\emph{,} V\emph{,}

Dec) is chosen-ciphertext simulatable when there exists a simulator Sim
such that the following

advantage is negligible for all PPT adversaries A:

