Note that this doesn't change

the way the zero-knowledge proof (c\emph{,} \textbf{z}) is computed, as
it was already computed through S2

since Game 1. This game is indistinguishable from Game 1 under the
IND-CPA security of

the Ring-LWE encryption scheme in Section 2.5. Namely, given an
adversary \emph{A} such that

Pr {[}\textbf{Game-1}{]} \emph{−}Pr {[}\textbf{Game-2}{]} is
non-negligible, consider the following IND-CPA adversary \emph{B}2.

On input public key (a\emph{,} t2), \emph{B}2 chooses s1\emph{,}
s2\emph{←S}1, computes t1 \emph{←}as1 + s2, and runs \emph{A} on public
key (a\emph{,} t1\emph{,} t2). When \emph{A} submits the language member
\emph{x} and witness \emph{w} to be encrypted, \emph{B}2 submits
\emph{x} and 1\emph{k}as its two challenge messages to obtain a
challenge ciphertext (\textbf{v}2\emph{,} \textbf{w}2).

It computes (\textbf{v}1\emph{,} \textbf{w}1) as an encryption of
\emph{w} under public key (a\emph{,} t1), uses the zero-knowledge

simulator S2 to generate a proof (c\emph{,} \textbf{z}), and hands
(\textbf{v}1\emph{,} \textbf{w}1\emph{,} \textbf{v}2\emph{,}
\textbf{w}2\emph{,} c\emph{,} \textbf{z}) as challenge ciphertext

to \emph{A}.

It is clear that if \emph{B}2's challenge ciphertext is an encryption of
\emph{w}, then \emph{B}2 provides \emph{A} with a perfect simulation of
Game 1, while if it is an encryption of 1\emph{k}, it provides a perfect
simulation

of Game 2. The advantage of \emph{B}2 in breaking the IND-CPA security
of the Ring-LWE encryption

scheme is therefore

\emph{ϵ}2 = 1 2\emph{·}Pr {[}\textbf{Game-1}{]} \emph{−}Pr
{[}\textbf{Game-2}{]} (28)
\end{quote}

