\textbf{6.2 Verifiably Encrypted Signatures}

Suppose two parties want to engage in a contract together and exchange
signatures on the agreed contract. Neither of the parties wants to be
the first to send his signature, however, fearing that the other party
may not reciprocate and hold the first party liable to the conditions in
the contract, without being held liable himself. Fair signature exchange
protocols {[}ASW00,BDM98{]} ensure that no party can obtain a
significant advantage over the other party by aborting the protocol
early.

Verifiably encrypted signatures {[}ASW00,BDM98,BGLS03{]} are an
important tool to build op-timistic fair exchange protocols. The first
party initially sends his signature encrypted under the key of a trusted
adjudicator such that the other party can verify that the ciphertext
indeed con-tains a valid signature on the agreed contract, but cannot
recover the signature itself. The second party responds by sending his
signature, after which the first party also sends over his signature. In
case the first party refuses to send his signature in the last step, the
second party can contact the adjudicator to have the encrypted signature
from the first decrypted.

We show how our relaxed verifiable encryption scheme can be used to
build verifiably encryp-ted signatures for the ring-based variant of
Gentry-Peikert-Vaikuntanathan (Ring-GPV) signature scheme {[}GPV08{]}
based on the hardness of the Ring-SIS or NTRU problems {[}SS11,DLP14{]}.
Here, the signer's public key is a polynomial b \emph{∈Rp}, while the
secret key is a trapdoor allowing to find, for a given u \emph{∈Rp},
short polynomials m1\emph{,} m2 such that bm1 + m2 = u. A signature on a
mes-sage \emph{µ} in the usual scheme is a short vector (m1\emph{,} m2)
such that bm1 + m2 = H(\emph{µ}) mod \emph{p}, where H :
\emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗→Rp} is a random oracle. It is easy to
show, however, that the scheme remains secure if one relaxes the
verification algorithm to accept any tuple of short polynomials
(m1\emph{,} m2\emph{,} c) such that bm1 + m2 = cH(\emph{µ}) mod
\emph{p}.

In the usual security proof, when the adversary produces a forgery,
bm1+m2 = H(\emph{µ}) mod \emph{p}, the 2= H(\emph{µ}) mod \emph{p}, and
thus obtains a solution simulator already possesses another equality
bm\emph{′}1+ m\emph{′}\\
1) + to Ring-SIS as b(m1 \emph{−}m\emph{′}a forgery bm1 + m2 =
cH(\emph{µ}) mod \emph{p}, then the simulator can obtain the equation
b(cm1 \emph{−}m\emph{′}(cm2 \emph{−}m\emph{′}For this modified signature
scheme that we build a verifiably encrypted signature scheme using 2) =
0 mod \emph{p}, which is still a (slightly longer) solution to Ring-SIS.
1) + (m2 \emph{−}m\emph{′}2) = 0 mod \emph{p}. If, on the other hand,
the adversary produces

our CCA-secure relaxed verifiable encryption scheme from Section 5.
Namely, to encrypt an honest signature (m1\emph{,} m2\emph{,} 1) under
the adjudicator's public key, one encrypts the witness \textbf{m} = {[}
m1 ; m2 {]} with the encryption scheme from Section 5 while proving that
{[} b 1 {]} \textbf{m} = H(\emph{µ}) mod \emph{p}. When the adjudicator
decrypts the signature, it recovers ( ¯\textbf{m}\emph{,}¯c) such that
{[} b 1 {]} ¯\textbf{m} = ¯cH(\emph{µ}) mod \emph{p}, which is also a
valid signature on \emph{µ}. Unforgeability follows from the
unforgeability of the relaxed Ring-GPV scheme, while the security
against extraction follows from the security of Ring-LWE encryption.

\textbf{6.3 Other Applications}

One of the most prominent applications of verifiable encryption is in
group signatures {[}CvH91{]}, where group members can sign anonymously
in name of the entire group, but their anonymity can be lifted by a
dedicated opening authority. A common construction paradigm
{[}CL06,BCK+14{]} is to let a user's signing key consist of a signature
by the group manager on the user's identity. To sign a message, the user
encrypts his identity under the public key of the opener and creates a
NIZK proof of knowledge of a valid signature for the encrypted identity.
To recover the identity of the signer, the opener simply decrypts the
ciphertext included in the signature.

